is  a utility used to automatically distribute labels between two
MPLS LSRs conforming to RFC5036.   Currently  some  features  de‐
scribed by RFC5036 are not fully implemented (see section).  sup‐
ports  peering  with  IPv6 LDP speakers and IPv6 labels exchange,
conforming to specifications in RFC5036 and  draft‐ietf‐mpls‐ldp‐
ipv6.   logs  information  using the interface.  You can increase
the log verbosity using the and flags.  Administrators can use to
connect to the control port (default: 2626) and use  this  inter‐
face  in  order to get informations about protocol status, neigh‐
bours et cetera but also to set runtime parameters.  The password
required for connecting is the same as the root  password.   com‐
putes  existing routes and tries to match them on MPLS labels an‐
nounced by other LDP peers.  This means that usual IP routes will
be changed into tagged routes, and MPLS  routing  table  will  be
populated.  Any change in MPLS topology will also be announced to
LDP  neighbors.   uses  currently Independent Control Mapping and
Downstream Unsolicited mode for Label Advertisment.  will  listen
on  a  route socket and compute the necessary changes in order to
change untagged routes into tagged routes.  This means  that  one
may  use  one’s favourite dynamic routing protocol daemon without
modifications.  The options are as follows: Specifies a  path  to
the  config  file.  Default: ‐ see for configuration file format.
Enable debug mode.  Don’t use route interception code.  will  not
make  any  changes  to routing table if started with this option.
Run in foreground.  Use STDOUT for warning  and  debug  messages.
Outputs  supported flags.  Changes the TCP control port (default:
2626).  Enable output of warning messages.  The command  appeared
in  doesn’t implement Path Vector and Hop Count TLVs.  As a secu‐
rity measure you SHOULD filter the LDP well‐known (646)  TCP  and
UDP ports using your favourite packet filter before starting This
is  the  current way used to filter neighbours and to protect the
system from possible external attacks like route injections.
